# === SYSCALL/SYSRET Hardware Interface ===
# SYSCALL: RCX=retRIP, R11=retRFLAGS, rax=num, rdi=a1, rsi=a2, rdx=a3

var syscall_entry_buf[256];  # Enlarged for IBRS instructions
# 1.46.x SMP sub-bite 4b: the SYSCALL entry path's two baked values — the user-RSP save slot
# and the kernel-stack top — go PER-CPU. The old single `kernel_rsp_save` / `syscall_kstack_top`
# globals were a hard single-core invariant: two CPUs in the stub at once would clobber one save
# slot and grow down one stack. The stub now indexes these by the (capped) APIC cpu id it RE-READS
# at each consumer (r8/rax * 8 + &array), so each CPU has its own slot/stack with NO register
# assumed to survive `call syscall_handler`. module-global `var X[4]` = 4*u64 = 32 bytes each.
#
# PD[0..127]-RESIDENCY (checkable): the per-CPU arrays the stub indexes live in kernel .bss (<16 MB, PD[0..7]),
# copied into every per-proc CR3 by the proc.cyr PD[0..127] copy, so the stub (running under the still-user CR3
# while KPTI is collapsed) reaches them. The kernel stacks themselves are region-7 slots reached through the
# DIRECT MAP (PDPT[8], mirrored into every per-proc CR3), never their identity VA.
# ⭐ 1.57.7 (Path 2, S3.4, stub bite C): the two per-CPU cells the entry used to write BESIDES the kernel-stack top
# are GONE. pcpu_kernel_rsp_save (the user-RSP entry scratch) — the stub now keeps the user RSP in r10 across the
# stack switch and pushes it straight into the process's own frame ([ktop-8]). pcpu_sc_entry_regs (the 1.44.16
# per-CPU capture of rcx/r11/rbx/rbp/r12-r15, read by the abandon-frame #44 and by fork#96 through sc_scr_base) —
# the voluntary switch saves the whole frame instead, and fork reads the caller's OWN syscall frame (SCF_*, below),
# which no other syscall on this CPU can overwrite and which stays valid across a switch.
# ⭐⭐ 1.57.6 (Path 2, S3.3) — pcpu_syscall_kstack_top[cpu] = THIS CPU's CURRENT PROC's KERNEL-STACK TOP: a
# mirror of TSS.RSP0 the stub can index with r8*8 (the TSS stride of 104 is not a SIB scale).
# ⛔ WHAT IT WAS: a per-CPU PRIMARY syscall stack (region 7, 0xF10000 + cpu*0x10000) that every process on
# the CPU shared, plus a SECOND one (0xF90000 + cpu*0x10000, `pcpu_syscall_kstack_top2`) that execwait#37
# swapped in so its child's syscalls could not grow down through the parent's suspended #37 frame and
# SYSRET cells. Both are gone. A syscall now runs on the CALLING PROCESS'S OWN kernel stack — the per-proc
# region-7 slot TSS.RSP0 already pointed at (proc.cyr proc_rsp0 / KSTACK_POOL_BASE) — so its SYSRET state
# lives in that process's frame (SCF_* below) whichever CPU finishes the syscall, and a #37 child's
# syscalls grow down the child's stack by construction.
# Written ONLY by kstack_install (below) — on every path that makes a proc current before ring 3 can run:
# do_context_switch (both switch paths: the timer's and, since 1.57.7, the voluntary int 0xE0 one — the retired
# sys_sched_yield's tail was a third), exec_and_wait, execwait#37's resume, kernel_resume.
# syscall_kstack_reserve seeds every slot with 0, so a SYSCALL that arrives before ANY install on its CPU faults on
# its first push instead of silently sharing some other process's stack.
# ⛔ 1.57.6 (S3-fix) — THAT IS FAIL-STOP, AND THIS LINE USED TO CALL IT "FAIL-LOUD". The push faults, the #PF
# cannot be pushed on the same RSP either, and it escalates to #DF on IST1. Mutation M-B2 (the top store skipped)
# measured the result under QEMU: a silent wedge, no serial line at all — the vector-8 stub reached only CMOS 0x54
# and the FB bar. The #DF stub now prints one COM1 line first ("PANIC: Double Fault ...", exc_df_report, idt.cyr).
# ⚠ AND A ZERO TOP COVERS ONLY THE FIRST ENTRY ON A CPU. The likely regression — a new switch path that forgets
# kstack_install — leaves the top naming the PREVIOUS process's stack, which is valid memory: nothing faults and
# the syscall runs on someone else's frames. kstack_check_entry (sched.cyr), the first call in syscall_handler, is
# what catches that one.
var pcpu_syscall_kstack_top[4];
# 1.46.x 4b: idempotency guard for syscall_kstack_reserve (was the old `syscall_kstack_top != 0`
# self-check, which the per-CPU arrays can't double as — slot 0 being 0 is now a valid mid-init state).
var syscall_kstack_reserved = 0;
# Saved kernel context for returning from user mode on exit.
# 1.46.x SMP STEP-2 (#3, 2026-06-26): PER-CPU. kernel_return_rsp (==1 = "a resume context is armed")
# is set by exec_and_wait, checked in exit#0, and zeroed by kernel_resume — all in the context of the
# foreground proc's (pinned) CPU. Two CPUs each running a foreground exec would clobber one another's
# armed flag (CPU A's exit() seeing CPU B's 0 → never longjmps → agnsh never resumes kybernet). Per-CPU
# cell (cpu c at byte c*8); pcpu_cpu() cpu_count<2 fast path = slot 0 → single-core byte-identical.
var pcpu_kernel_return_rsp[4];
fn kernel_return_rsp_set(v) { store64(&pcpu_kernel_return_rsp + pcpu_cpu() * 8, v); return 0; }
fn kernel_return_rsp_get() { return load64(&pcpu_kernel_return_rsp + pcpu_cpu() * 8); }
var pcpu_kernel_return_rip[4];
fn kernel_return_rip_set(v) { store64(&pcpu_kernel_return_rip + pcpu_cpu() * 8, v); return 0; }
fn kernel_return_rip_get() { return load64(&pcpu_kernel_return_rip + pcpu_cpu() * 8); }
var ibrs_supported = 0;  # S9: set to 1 if IBRS is available
# 1.41.3 (a4=r10): the syscall's 4th argument (rename/link need four). The entry
# stub preserves the user's r10 into r9 (the SysV 6th-arg register) before the
# CR3-switch scratch destroys r10, so it arrives in syscall_handler as sc_arg4, which stashes it where the
# 4-arg ksyscall reads it — keeping ksyscall's arity (and its many in-kernel 3-arg callers) unchanged.
# ⭐ 1.57.7 (Path 2, S3.4): that cell is PER PROCESS now — proc_sc_a4[pid] with ksyscall_a4_store/set/get
# (proc.cyr). It was per-CPU (pcpu_ksyscall_a4, 1.46.x) and before that one global; per-CPU was right while a
# syscall ran start to finish on one CPU, and wrong the moment a syscall can BLOCK: it resumes, maybe on another
# CPU, after other processes' syscalls have run on both.
# 1.46.x SMP sub-bite 4c: the KPTI CR3 pair the stub installs on entry (kernel CR3) and exit
# (user CR3), now PER-CPU. The old single `kpti_kernel_cr3` / `kpti_user_cr3` globals (proc.cyr)
# were a single-core invariant — one CPU's scheduler CR3 update would steer another CPU's stub
# CR3 install. Each is `var X[4]` = 4 u64 (cpu c at byte c*8). The stub bakes the per-CPU base
# via `mov r10,[r8*8+disp32]` (r8 = capped APIC cpu); the C-side writers/reader index by
# pcpu_cpu()*8. KPTI is currently COLLAPSED (kernel==user==running CR3) so the installs are
# same-value no-ops today, but the per-CPU plumbing is correct for when KPTI un-collapses.
var pcpu_kpti_kernel_cr3[4];
var pcpu_kpti_user_cr3[4];

# C-level handler called from the syscall entry stub.
# user_rsp = the caller's RSP (saved before switching to kernel stack)
fn syscall_handler(user_rsp, sc_num, sc_arg1, sc_arg2, sc_arg3, sc_arg4) {
    # 1.57.6 (S3-fix): ONE cpu-id read serves both entry duties (it is a LAPIC MMIO read once cpu_count >= 2 — a VM
    # exit under KVM; a second one here measured +20% on getpid at -smp 4). IF=0 until the arm runs: no migration.
    # 1.57.7 (S3.4): kstack_check_entry hands back the pid it loads from pcpu_curproc (-1 out of range), so the
    # per-PROCESS a4 store costs no second LAPIC read either.
    var sh_cpu = pcpu_cpu();
    var sh_me = kstack_check_entry(sh_cpu);            # is this CPU's syscall top the CALLER's stack? (sched.cyr) -> the caller's pid
    ksyscall_a4_store(sh_me, sc_arg4);                 # proc_sc_a4[caller] (proc.cyr)
    # sched_yield(#44): the in-kernel yield-or-park (sched.cyr sys_sched_yield_k → sched_yield_or_halt: the voluntary
    # switch to a READY process, else one hlt as a designated preempt point — 1.57.7). Dispatched here, not in ksyscall,
    # so an in-kernel ksyscall(44) keeps falling to its default -1: kernel code that wants to yield calls
    # sched_yield_kernel (or sched_yield_or_halt) directly.
    # ⭐ 1.57.7 (S7) — ONE EXIT. Every dispatch leaves through proc_b1_check(sh_me) (sched.cyr): the lifecycle boundary
    # (C) "B1". A pending kill ends the process here (never returns); a pending stop PARKS it here with `result` kept
    # on its own kernel stack (after SIGCONT the syscall returns its real value); a deferred covered-console spill
    # (klug_spill_owed) runs here too. Fast path: 3 loads + 1 call. syscall_hw.cyr is parsed before proc.cyr, so the
    # lifecycle arrays are read inside proc_b1_check.
    var result = 0;
    # sched_yield(#44): the in-kernel yield-or-park — see above. The INV-FG-1 tripwire stays (PLAN S7-E1).
    if (sc_num == 44) { fg_if0_tripwire(sh_me); result = sys_sched_yield_k(); }
    else {
        # ⛔ fork#96 dispatches HERE: it needs `user_rsp`, which only this function is handed, and the child's resume
        # registers come from the CALLER's own syscall frame (sc_frame_get), which exists only on a path reached from
        # the ring-3 entry stub. A `ksyscall(96)` from in-kernel code has no such frame.
        if (sc_num == 96) { result = sys_fork(user_rsp); }
        else {
            # pause(#14) is cyrius _agnos_sock_recv_block's poll-backoff between non-blocking sock_recv#49 polls, so it
            # must YIELD to other ready procs (a bare/preempt-disabled hlt starves them — the two-proc setu-present
            # freeze, 1.53.9). sys_pause (sched.cyr): KSTACK_SELFTEST's probe hook first (a no-op twin in production),
            # then the in-kernel yield-or-park (1.57.7) — to a READY proc, else ONE IF=1 hlt at preempt_count 0 (device
            # IRQs and a 0xE1 kick end it; not charged) — and only when that is refused ksyscall(14)'s legacy hlt.
            if (sc_num == 14) { fg_if0_tripwire(sh_me); result = sys_pause(sc_arg1, sc_arg2, sc_arg3); }   # INV-FG-1
            else { result = ksyscall(sc_num, sc_arg1, sc_arg2, sc_arg3); }
        }
    }
    # ⭐ 1.57.7 (S8) — THE ONE CONSUME SITE of the spawn_limits#107 arm: every success, failure and refusal return of
    # #3/#37/#43 passes here (for #37 after P wakes: harmless, P cannot spawn while blocked and placement happened at
    # load). sh_me, not proc_current_get(): a per-process fact valid across a block or migration inside the dispatch.
    # In-kernel ksyscall(3/37/43) bypasses this — inert: pid 0 never arms outside ktest K2, which clears its own arm.
    if (sc_num == 3 || sc_num == 37 || sc_num == 43) { lim_arm_clear(sh_me); }
    proc_b1_check(sh_me);                              # sched.cyr: may park (a stop) or never return (a kill)
    return result;
}


# === fork#96 (1.56.54) ============================================================================
#
# Duplicate the calling process. Returns the CHILD's pid to the parent and **0 to the child**, or -1
# on failure. `user_rsp` is `syscall_handler`'s own first parameter — the caller's RSP, saved by the
# entry stub before the kernel-stack switch.
#
# ⛔⛔ THIS MUST BE DISPATCHED FROM `syscall_handler`, NOT FROM `ksyscall`, AND THAT IS NOT STYLE.
# ⭐ 1.57.7 (Path 2, S3.4): the child's resume context comes from the PARENT'S OWN SYSCALL FRAME at the top of its
# kernel stack (sc_frame_get(parent, SCF_*), the layout below) — the caller's rcx (post-SYSCALL RIP), r11 (RFLAGS),
# user RSP and callee-saved set, pushed by the entry stub. That frame exists only on a path reached from the
# ring-3 stub, and it needs `user_rsp`, which only syscall_handler is handed. Until 1.57.7 the source was the
# per-CPU pcpu_sc_entry_regs capture (sc_scr_base), overwritten by the next syscall on this CPU — wrong the moment
# the parent's syscall can be switched out mid-flight; that capture block is gone from the stub.
#
# ⭐ WHY THE REGISTER SET IS THIS ONE AND NOT ALL SIXTEEN. Across a SYSCALL the caller-saved registers
# (rcx, rdx, rsi, rdi, r8-r11) are clobbered by the ABI — cyrius models the inline `0F 05` as
# clobbering them, so no ring-3 program can have a live value there. What must survive is the
# callee-saved set (rbx, rbp, r12-r15) plus rsp/rip/rflags, and that is precisely what the stub
# pushes. Zeroing the rest is honest: it matches what the parent's own syscall return will do.
#
# ⭐ 1.57.7 — THE CHILD'S RFLAGS ALWAYS HAS IF SET. A forked child is an ordinary scheduled process, even when its
# parent runs IF=0 (a foreground exec child, the pre-scheduler model): the child is published READY and resumed by
# a scheduler iretq, and an IF=0 ring-3 child would never take a tick — nothing could ever switch it out or wake a
# halt it made. fork-smoke's foreground-parent arm (FORK_SELFTEST's `run /bin/forker`) checks FORKER-CHILD-IF=1.
#
# ⚠ THE CHILD'S rax IS 0 AND THE PARENT'S IS THE CHILD PID — the entire fork contract, and the only
# place the two diverge. The parent gets its value by returning it from here as normal.
fn sys_fork(user_rsp) {
    var parent = proc_current_get();
    var src_cr3 = proc_get_cr3(parent);
    if (src_cr3 == 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    if (src_cr3 == 0x1000) { return 0 - 1; }      # a kernel-CR3 caller is not a forkable ring-3 proc

    # ⚠ ALLOCATE THE SLOT BEFORE THE ADDRESS SPACE. proc_dup_address_space can copy many megabytes;
    # discovering the table is full afterwards would mean tearing all of it down again.
    var child = proc_alloc_slot();
    if (child < 0) { return 0 - 1; }              # process table full
    # ⭐ 1.57.7 (S7): the parent INCARNATION is recorded FIRST (moved up from below the frame copy), so the birth check
    # sees it: a caller with a pending kill creates no child, and a tree-stopped caller's child is born stop-pending
    # (proc_birth_check, sched.cyr — the same rule as proc_create_user). proc_alloc_slot's scrub set ppid 0.
    store64(&proc_ppid + child * 8, parent);
    store64(&proc_ppid_epoch + child * 8, proc_epoch_get(parent));
    if (proc_birth_check(child, parent) == 0) { proc_slot_release_unborn(child); return 0 - 1; }
    # 1.57.7 (S8): the child inherits the parent's CAPS (never its arm — the alloc reset ran with child != current)
    # and continues its HIGH-ARENA CURSOR (its copied high pages sit below it; a fresh cursor started at the floor,
    # on those copies). No cap check: the child's footprint equals the parent's, which is <= the parent's cap. A capped
    # fork child starts at 0 ticks with the same CPU cap (budgets are per process).
    lim_copy(child, parent);
    himmap_cursor_copy(child, parent);

    var dst_cr3 = proc_dup_address_space(src_cr3);
    if (dst_cr3 == 0) {
        # ⚠ Hand the slot back. proc_alloc_slot marked it CLAIMING(3); leaving it there would leak a
        # process-table entry on every failed fork, and the table is 16 deep.
        # ⛔ 1.57.7 (S7, D-10): through proc_slot_release_unborn, NOT proc_set_state(child, 0): with the ppid now set
        # early, a bare state 0 would be a PHANTOM ZOMBIE that waitpid(-1) would "reap" with a stale exit code.
        proc_slot_release_unborn(child);
        return 0 - 1;
    }

    var p = &proc_table + child * 176;
    store64(p, child);                             # pid
    store64(p + 8, 3);                             # state 3 = CLAIMING — not schedulable yet
    store64(p + 16, user_rsp);                     # rsp  — the parent's own user stack pointer (== sc_frame_get(parent, SCF_URSP))
    store64(p + 24, sc_frame_get(parent, SCF_RIP)); # rip — the frame's rcx = post-SYSCALL return address
    memset(p + 32, 0, 128);                        # every GPR field zeroed, then the live ones below
    # ⛔⛔ 1.56.55 — THE CHILD'S RETURN VALUE GOES TO p+112, NOT p+32, AND p+32 IS r11.
    # sched.cyr states it as a load-bearing invariant (the REVERSED-LABEL SLOT MAP, directly above resched_handler
    # since 1.57.7): *"REVERSED-LABEL SLOT MAP … the proc-slot
    # caller-saved fields restore into the REVERSE of their labels (derived from the ISR pop order,
    # pic.cyr) … p+32→r11, p+48→r10, p+56→r9, p+64→r8, p+72→rdi, p+88→rsi, p+96→rdx, p+104→rcx,
    # p+112→rax. So the rax=0 return value goes to p+112 (NOT p+32)."* The labels in the `struct
    # Process` doc-comment are correct for the SAVE side and for the symmetric timer round-trip, which
    # is why nothing has ever noticed; they are wrong for any row written BY HAND, which is exactly
    # what fork does.
    # ⚠ 1.56.54 wrote the 0 to p+32 and the child still saw 0 — **by accident**, because the memset
    # above zeroes p+112 as well. Measured: mutating p+32 to 99 left the child reading 0, and the gate
    # stayed green. So the fork contract was correct by coincidence and would have put any non-zero
    # value into r11. Written explicitly now, so it is intentional and a mutation is detectable.
    store64(p + 112, 0);                           # rax (reversed map) = THE CHILD`S FORK RETURN
    store64(p + 40,  sc_frame_get(parent, SCF_RBX));   # rbx
    store64(p + 80,  sc_frame_get(parent, SCF_RBP));   # rbp
    store64(p + 120, sc_frame_get(parent, SCF_R12));   # r12
    store64(p + 128, sc_frame_get(parent, SCF_R13));   # r13
    store64(p + 136, sc_frame_get(parent, SCF_R14));   # r14
    store64(p + 144, sc_frame_get(parent, SCF_R15));   # r15
    store64(p + 152, sc_frame_get(parent, SCF_RFL) | 0x200);   # rflags = the frame's r11, IF FORCED (see the banner)
    store64(p + 160, dst_cr3);                     # cr3 — the duplicate, NOT the parent's
    store64(p + 168, 0);                           # exit_code

    # ⚠ RING-3 SELECTORS, or the child resumes at CPL0 on the parent's user RIP. proc_alloc_slot leaves
    # the ring-0 defaults from proc_create_user's shape; a forked child is ring 3 by construction.
    proc_set_ring3(child);
    # (ppid + ppid_epoch were stored above, before the birth check — 1.57.7 S7.)
    # ⭐ THE CHILD INHERITS A COPY OF THE PARENT'S fd TABLE — open files, pipes and sockets survive the
    # fork, which is the entire point for a fork-per-connection server: the accepted socket must be in
    # the child. Same single inheritance point every other user proc uses.
    vfs_fd_inherit(child, parent);
    # ⭐ AND ITS NAME — a forked child is the same program, so `proclist`#99 should say so rather than
    # leaving the slot's freshly-scrubbed empty name. Copied byte-wise from the parent's 32-byte cell.
    var cn_dst = proc_name_ptr(child);
    var cn_src = proc_name_ptr(parent);
    if (cn_dst != 0) {
        if (cn_src != 0) { memcpy(cn_dst, cn_src, 32); }
    }

    # Schedulable only now that the row is complete.
    proc_set_state(child, 1);
    return child;
}

# 1.40.5: reserve the SYSCALL kernel stack (a fresh 2 MB region; RSP set near
# its top, grows down). Call EARLY in boot (right after heap_init) so the first
# pmm_alloc_2mb pins it to phys 0x200000 → identity VA 0x3F0000, BELOW the user
# code/stack VAs (0x400000+); a late alloc could land at 0x400000 and collide
# with the user-code page (the ~17% exec flake). Mapped identity (within the
# 0-1 GB the per-process CR3 mirrors), writable. Idempotent.
fn syscall_kstack_reserve() {
    if (syscall_kstack_reserved != 0) { return 0; }
    # 1.46.x 4b: FIXED per-CPU kstacks whose PHYS lives in region 7 (0xE00000-0x1000000, 14-16 MB),
    # reserved as kernel in pmm_init. The PRIMARY phys tops are 0xF10000 + cpu*0x10000; the SECOND
    # (execwait #37 nested) phys tops are 0xF90000 + cpu*0x10000. All sit ABOVE the proc_rsp0 pool
    # (phys 0xE00000-0xF00000) and 64 KB apart per CPU, so no two CPUs' stacks — and neither of a
    # CPU's two stacks — ever overlap. **1.51.x: the stub loads RSP from these table entries via the
    # DIRECT-MAP VA (DIRECTMAP_BASE + phys, 8 GB+), NOT the low identity VA.** Region 7's identity VA
    # (14-16 MB) is inside the user-segment range (elf_load maps PT_LOADs to 256 MB), so a large binary
    # like ark (segment to ~20 MB) overrode PD[7] in its per-proc CR3 and clobbered the syscall kstack
    # -> the exec path #PF'd at SP=0xF10000 (CPL0) -> #DF -> triple fault. The direct-map (PDPT[8..71],
    # mirrored into every per-proc CR3, vmm.cyr) is above ALL user VA and never segment-overridden, so
    # the kstack is always reachable. The stub's load is `mov rsp, [r8*8 + &table]` (a 64-bit load), so
    # storing 8 GB+ VAs here needs NO stub change. Phys stays region-7-reserved (same determinism as the
    # old fixed tops, no pmm_alloc_2mb next-free flake; 1.40.9).
    # ⛔ 1.57.6 (Path 2, S3.3) — EVERYTHING ABOVE IS HISTORY: the tops start at 0 (fail-STOP — see
    # pcpu_syscall_kstack_top for what a zero top does and does not catch). There is no
    # shared per-CPU syscall stack any more (see pcpu_syscall_kstack_top); every ring-3 entry is preceded by
    # kstack_install(pid), which points this CPU's slot at the entering proc's own region-7 stack. The old
    # fixed tops' phys (0xF00000-0xF40000 and 0xF80000-0xFC0000) are the kthread pool and a FREE range now —
    # gdt.cyr carries the region-7 map. The direct-map rule above still holds for every stack in region 7.
    for (var c = 0; c < 4; c = c + 1) {
        store64(&pcpu_syscall_kstack_top + c * 8, 0);
    }
    syscall_kstack_reserved = 1;
    return 0;
}

# ═══════════════ 1.57.6 (Path 2, S3.3) — THE SYSCALL FRAME: per-PROCESS, at the top of its kernel stack ═══════════════
# Live only while that proc is inside a SYSCALL from ring 3 (the stub builds it; ktop = proc_rsp0_top(pid)):
#   [ktop-8]  user RSP      [ktop-16] user RBP      [ktop-24] user RIP (rcx)      [ktop-32] user RFLAGS (r11)
#   [ktop-40] rbx   [ktop-48] r12   [ktop-56] r13   [ktop-64] r14   [ktop-72] r15
# The exit POPS rbx/r12-r15, r11, rcx, rbp and loads RSP from [ktop-8], so SYSRET state belongs to the
# process, not to whichever CPU ends the syscall. (The IBRS-set block between push rbp and push rcx is
# push/pop balanced, so it never moves a slot.)
var SCF_URSP = 8;
var SCF_RBP  = 16;
var SCF_RIP  = 24;
var SCF_RFL  = 32;
var SCF_RBX  = 40;
var SCF_R12  = 48;
var SCF_R13  = 56;
var SCF_R14  = 64;
var SCF_R15  = 72;
fn sc_frame_get(pid, off) { return load64(proc_rsp0_top(pid) - off); }

# Make `pid`'s kernel stack THE kernel stack of this CPU: TSS.RSP0 (CPL3 interrupts) and the syscall top
# (SYSCALL entry) — one stack for both, because a proc is either in ring 3 (an interrupt frame starts at the
# top) or in a syscall (the frame starts at the top and any interrupt nests below it), never both at once.
# ⚠ Call it under a CR3 that maps the LAPIC (every CR3 this kernel builds does): tss_set_rsp0 reads the id.
fn kstack_install(pid) {
    var top = proc_rsp0_top(pid);
    tss_set_rsp0(top);
    store64(&pcpu_syscall_kstack_top + pcpu_cpu() * 8, top);
    return 0;
}

# 1.46.x SMP sub-bite 4b: capped APIC cpu id for the C-side per-CPU SYSCALL-stack readers
# (sched.cyr:348 / the ew37 save+restore+swap in syscall.cyr). Mirrors proc.cyr's accessor
# pattern (cpu_count<2 fast path = slot 0, no APIC read → byte-for-byte the old single-core global;
# >=4 caps to slot 0 since tss_get_current_cpu_id() is uncapped). The STUB caps independently via
# its own cmp r8,4;jb;xor — both paths must cap (CPU-CAP invariant).
fn pcpu_cpu() {
    if (cpu_count < 2) { return 0; }
    var c = tss_get_current_cpu_id();
    if (c >= 4) { c = 0; }
    return c;
}

# 1.46.x SMP sub-bite 4a: per-CPU SYSCALL MSRs (STAR/LSTAR/SFMASK/EFER). The BSP runs this via
# syscall_init; an AP will run it DIRECTLY at bring-up (sub-bite 6) without rebuilding the shared
# stub. LSTAR points at syscall_entry_buf, which syscall_stub_build (BSP-once) fills. No [rbp-N]
# hand-asm here (the LSTAR `var v=VALUE; asm{…rax…}` idiom is register-based), so the split is
# frame-offset-safe; the only [rbp-N] hardcode (the IBRS cpuid capture) stays in syscall_init.
fn syscall_msr_init() {
    # Program the MSRs for SYSCALL/SYSRET.
    # MSR 0xC0000081 (STAR): segment selectors
    #   Bits 47:32 = kernel CS selector (0x08) — for SYSCALL entry
    #   Bits 63:48 = user CS/SS base — SYSRET computes CS=base+16, SS=base+8. MUST be 0x13
    #     (= 0x10 | 3, RPL-3 baked in), NOT 0x10. On AMD (APM Vol 3 §SYSRET) 64-bit SYSRET forces
    #     CPL/CS.RPL=3 but does NOT force SS.RPL — SS is loaded VERBATIM as base+8. With base=0x10
    #     SS=0x18 (RPL 0) while CPL=3, so the NEXT timer-ISR iretq returning to ring 3 #GP(0x18)'d on
    #     real Zen (SS.RPL != CS.RPL). Intel/QEMU OR 3 into SS, so it was invisible there — the iron-only
    #     IF=1-agnsh blocker. base=0x13 → CS=0x13+16=0x23, SS=0x13+8=0x1B, both RPL 3 (the +8/+16 don't
    #     carry into the low 2 bits, so the baked RPL survives). Exactly Linux's MSR_STAR layout trick.
    # MSR 0xC0000082 (LSTAR): handler RIP (64-bit)
    # MSR 0xC0000084 (SFMASK): RFLAGS bits to clear on SYSCALL (mask IF so interrupts off in kernel)

    # LSTAR = address of our syscall entry stub
    var lstar_val = &syscall_entry_buf;

    # wrmsr: ecx=MSR#, edx:eax=value (edx=high32, eax=low32)

    # Write STAR (MSR 0xC0000081)
    # mov ecx, 0xC0000081; mov eax, low32(star); mov edx, high32(star); wrmsr
    asm {
        0xB9; 0x81; 0x00; 0x00; 0xC0;
        0xB8; 0x00; 0x00; 0x00; 0x00;
        0xBA; 0x08; 0x00; 0x13; 0x00;   # edx=0x00130008: STAR[63:48]=0x13 (RPL-3 baked → SYSRET SS=0x1B on AMD), STAR[47:32]=0x08
        0x0F; 0x30;
    }

    # Write LSTAR (MSR 0xC0000082) = &syscall_entry_buf (the SYSCALL entry RIP).
    # 1.40.3: the old path built the wrmsr as bytecode in a LOCAL (stack) buffer
    # and executed it via `var mfn = &msrbuf; asm { call rax }`. With EFER.NXE
    # set (EFER=0xd00), the stack is no-execute, so that call faulted and LSTAR
    # was never written — the first real SYSCALL then vectored to a stale/garbage
    # LSTAR (into data ~0x113 B before the stub) and triple-faulted. NOT a Cyrius
    # bug: the `var v = VALUE; asm {…rax…}` idiom itself is sound (the CR3
    # switches rely on it and reach ring 3); the bug was *executing NX stack
    # data*. Fixed with a direct wrmsr — `var lstar_v = lstar_val` lands the
    # address in rax, then split to edx:eax and wrmsr. No bytecode, no stack exec.
    var lstar_v = lstar_val;
    asm {
        0x48; 0x89; 0xC2;              # mov rdx, rax   (rax = lstar_val)
        0x48; 0xC1; 0xEA; 0x20;        # shr rdx, 32    (edx = high32)
        0xB9; 0x82; 0x00; 0x00; 0xC0;  # mov ecx, 0xC0000082 (LSTAR)
        0x0F; 0x30;                     # wrmsr (edx:eax -> LSTAR; eax = rax low32)
    }

    # Write SFMASK (MSR 0xC0000084) = 0x40700 (clear IF|TF|DF|AC on SYSCALL entry).
    # 1.42.14 SECURITY (audit #4/#5): masking ONLY IF (0x200) let a ring-3 caller
    # carry TF/DF/AC into ring 0 — TF=1 single-steps the kernel into the bare-iretq
    # #DB gate, DF=1 would reverse any future rep-string op into an OOB write, and
    # AC=1 pre-disables SMAP ahead of the stub's STAC. Clearing all four on entry
    # makes the kernel begin every syscall with single-step off, DF=0, and SMAP
    # enforced (0x40700 = IF 0x200 | TF 0x100 | DF 0x400 | AC 0x40000).
    asm {
        0xB9; 0x84; 0x00; 0x00; 0xC0;
        0xB8; 0x00; 0x07; 0x04; 0x00;
        0xBA; 0x00; 0x00; 0x00; 0x00;
        0x0F; 0x30;
    }

    # Also enable SCE (System Call Extensions) in IA32_EFER (MSR 0xC0000080)
    # Read current EFER, set bit 0 (SCE), write back
    asm {
        0xB9; 0x80; 0x00; 0x00; 0xC0;
        0x0F; 0x32;
        0x0D; 0x01; 0x00; 0x00; 0x00;
        0x0F; 0x30;
    }
    return 0;
}

# BSP boot entry: reserve the syscall kstack, program this CPU's MSRs, detect IBRS, then build the
# shared SYSCALL entry stub ONCE. `cpuid_edx` is the SOLE local here so it lands at [rbp-0x08] —
# the offset the IBRS-capture asm hardcodes (the spin_unlock sole-local pattern; objdump-verified).
# ⭐ 1.57.6 (Path 2, S3.3) — THE STUB IS BUILT ONCE AND IS IMMUTABLE AFTERWARDS. syscall_init used to rebuild
# it on EVERY call: main.cyr calls it at 13 selftest sites (one of them, fork_disk_selftest, after
# sched_active = 1 with the APs executing the stub) and exec_and_wait called it on every exec — rewriting
# bytes another CPU may be executing is cross-modifying code. Now the MSRs are (re)programmed every call and
# the IBRS probe + stub build run once; the per-exec caller (exec_and_wait) calls syscall_msr_init directly.
var syscall_stub_built = 0;
fn syscall_init() {
    syscall_kstack_reserve();   # no-op if already reserved early in boot
    syscall_msr_init();
    if (syscall_stub_built != 0) { return 0; }

    # S9: Check CPUID for IBRS support (leaf 7, subleaf 0, EDX bit 26)
    ibrs_supported = 0;
    var cpuid_edx = 0;
    asm {
        0xB8; 0x07; 0x00; 0x00; 0x00;   # mov eax, 7
        0x31; 0xC9;                       # xor ecx, ecx (subleaf 0)
        0x0F; 0xA2;                       # cpuid
        0x48; 0x89; 0x55; 0xF8;          # mov [rbp-0x08], rdx (cpuid_edx)
    }
    if ((cpuid_edx & (1 << 26)) != 0) {
        ibrs_supported = 1;
    }
    syscall_stub_build();
    syscall_stub_built = 1;
    return 0;
}

# BSP-ONCE: emit the shared SYSCALL entry stub into syscall_entry_buf. APs must NOT call this (they
# share the BSP-built buffer). Pure byte emission (store8/store64) — no [rbp-N] hand-asm, so the
# extraction is frame-offset-safe. (The syscall_stub_built once-guard + the ew37 rebuild removal
# are coupled, deferred to 4b per the adversarial review.)
fn syscall_stub_build() {
    # Build the SYSCALL entry stub in syscall_entry_buf.
    # On entry from SYSCALL:
    #   RCX = user RIP (return addr)
    #   R11 = user RFLAGS
    #   RAX = syscall number
    #   RDI = arg1, RSI = arg2, RDX = arg3
    #   RSP = still user RSP (SYSCALL doesn't switch stack!)
    #
    # We need to (1.57.6 Path 2 S3.3 — THE FRAME IS PER-PROCESS, the SCF_* block above has the layout; 1.57.7 S3.4
    # stub bite C — no per-CPU cell is touched between the stack switch and SYSRET):
    #   1. a4 (r10) -> r9; APIC id -> r8 (capped); kernel CR3 (via r10)
    #   2. Save user RSP in r10 (dead since the CR3 load)
    #   3. Switch to THIS PROCESS's kernel stack (pcpu_syscall_kstack_top[cpu], written by kstack_install)
    #   4. Build the frame: push r10 = user RSP [ktop-8], rbp [-16], (IBRS, balanced), rcx = user RIP [-24],
    #      r11 = user RFLAGS [-32], rbx [-40], r12 [-48], r13 [-56], r14 [-64], r15 [-72]
    #   5. Call syscall_handler(user_rsp = [rsp+0x40], num, arg1, arg2, arg3, a4)
    #   6. Pop r15..r12, rbx, r11, rcx, rbp; load RSP from the frame's [ktop-8] (mov rsp,[rsp])
    #   7. User CR3; SYSRET
    # FINAL REGISTER CONTRACT (1.57.7): in — rax num · rdi/rsi/rdx a1-a3 · r10 a4 · rcx user RIP · r11 user RFLAGS ·
    # rsp user RSP; scratch — r8 (cpu) · r9 (a4) · r10 (kernel CR3, then user RSP); out — rax · rcx/r11 the SYSRET
    # pair · rbx/rbp/r12-r15/rsp from the frame · rdx/rsi/rdi/r8-r10 hold handler leftovers.

    var ep = &syscall_entry_buf;
    var eoff = 0;
    # 1.46.x 4b: per-CPU base addresses for the disp32 of the r8*8 / rax*8 based loads/stores. Each
    # is the SINGLE u64 base &array; the index register (r8 or rax, holding the capped APIC cpu) and
    # the *8 SIB scale supply the per-CPU stride. EMITTED store32(+4), NOT store64(+8) — see the
    # DISP32-EMIT-WIDTH rule at each emit site.
    var kst_base  = &pcpu_syscall_kstack_top;  # mov rsp, [r8*8+disp32]  (kstack switch)
    # 1.46.x 4c: kcr3/ucr3 are loaded via `mov r10,[r8*8+disp32]` (r8 = capped APIC cpu). Both disp32s are EMITTED
    # via store32(+4), NOT store64(+8) — see the DISP32-EMIT-WIDTH rule at each site.
    var handler = &syscall_handler;
    var kcr3_addr = &pcpu_kpti_kernel_cr3;
    var ucr3_addr = &pcpu_kpti_user_cr3;

    # 1.41.3 (a4=r10): FIRST instruction — preserve the user's r10 (4th syscall
    # arg) into r9 before the CR3-switch scratch below overwrites r10. r9 is
    # untouched by the entire entry path (CR3 switch / stack switch / IBRS /
    # arg shuffle all avoid it), so it arrives at `call syscall_handler` as the
    # natural SysV 6th argument (sc_arg4). r9 is caller-saved, so clobbering it
    # is ABI-safe — same posture as the stub's existing r8 clobber.
    # mov r9, r10 — 4D 89 D1
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x4D); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x89); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xD1); eoff = eoff + 3;

    # 1.46.x SMP sub-bite 4b: ENTRY APIC-read-into-r8 — carry the capped APIC cpu id in r8 for the
    # duration of the entry window (R8-AS-CPU invariant: r8 survives untouched from here through the
    # kernel-CR3 load and the kernel-stack SWITCH below — the IBRS-on block does NOT
    # write r8 — first reused only at the live `mov r8,rdx` arg3 shuffle). The exit RE-READS the APIC
    # independently (no register assumed to survive IBRS / `call syscall_handler`). Reads the Local APIC ID register at 0xFEE00020 (the same MMIO
    # tss_get_current_cpu_id() uses), shifts/masks to the 8-bit APIC id, then caps to 0..3 (CPU-CAP).
    # mov r8, 0xFEE00020 — 49 B8 imm64
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x49); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0xB8); eoff = eoff + 2;
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x20); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xE0); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0xFE);
    store8(ep + eoff + 4, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 5, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 6, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 7, 0x00); eoff = eoff + 8;
    # mov r8d, [r8] — 45 8B 00   (load 32-bit APIC ID register; zero-extends into r8)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x45); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x8B); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0x00); eoff = eoff + 3;
    # shr r8, 24 — 49 C1 E8 18
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x49); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0xC1); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xE8); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0x18); eoff = eoff + 4;
    # and r8, 0xFF — 41 81 E0 FF 00 00 00
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x81); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xE0);
    store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0xFF); store8(ep + eoff + 4, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 5, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 6, 0x00); eoff = eoff + 7;
    # cmp r8, 4 — 49 83 F8 04
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x49); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x83); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xF8); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0x04); eoff = eoff + 4;
    # jb +3 — 72 03   (if r8 < 4, skip the 3-byte xor; JB-DISPLACEMENT: lands exactly past 45 31 C0)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x72); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x03); eoff = eoff + 2;
    # xor r8d, r8d — 45 31 C0   (cap out-of-range APIC id to slot 0)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x45); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x31); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xC0); eoff = eoff + 3;

    # S8 (KPTI): Switch from user CR3 to kernel CR3 FIRST.
    # We're ring-0 (SYSCALL changed CPL), user page tables have PD entry 0
    # mapped supervisor-only, so kernel code at 0-2MB is accessible.
    # 1.46.x 4c POINT 1: load THIS CPU's kernel CR3 from pcpu_kpti_kernel_cr3, install it. r8 holds the
    # capped APIC cpu (from the 4b entry block; untouched since; this runs under the STILL-user CR3, and
    # pcpu_kpti_kernel_cr3 is kernel .bss reachable via proc.cyr's PD[0..127] copy). The SIB load folds
    # the old `mov r10,imm64(&kpti_kernel_cr3); mov r10,[r10]` indirection into one based load.
    # Was 3 instr; now: mov r10,[r8*8 + &pcpu_kpti_kernel_cr3]; mov cr3,r10.
    # mov r10, [r8*8+disp32] — 4E 8B 14 C5 <disp32>
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x4E); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x8B); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0x14); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0xC5); eoff = eoff + 4;
    # DISP32-EMIT-WIDTH: store32(+4), NOT store64(+8). disp = the single u64 base &pcpu_kpti_kernel_cr3.
    store32(ep + eoff, kcr3_addr); eoff = eoff + 4;
    # mov cr3, r10 — 41 0F 22 DA (REX.B for r10; NOT 0x44 = REX.R → cr11 → #UD)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x0F); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0x22); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0xDA); eoff = eoff + 4;

    # ⭐ 1.57.7 (Path 2, S3.4, stub bite C) — SAVE the user RSP in r10: `mov r10, rsp` (49 89 E2). r10 is dead
    # here — its user value (a4) was ferried to r9 by the first instruction, and its kernel-CR3 value was consumed
    # by the `mov cr3, r10` above. It survives untouched to the `push r10` below (the stack switch writes only
    # RSP). This was `mov [r8*8 + &pcpu_kernel_rsp_save], rsp` (8 B) — a per-CPU cell, re-read by the push and by
    # the off-100 consumer; with the frame per-process, the per-CPU cell has no reader left and is gone.
    # (The 1.44.16 capture block that stood here — rcx/r11/rbx/rbp/r12-r15 into the per-CPU pcpu_sc_entry_regs
    # for the abandon-frame #44 and for fork — is DELETED, 63 B: the voluntary switch saves the whole frame, and
    # fork reads the caller's own syscall frame, SCF_*.)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x49); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x89); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xE2); eoff = eoff + 3;   # mov r10, rsp

    # 1.46.x 4b point4 SWITCH — switch RSP to THIS CPU's pcpu_syscall_kstack_top (the CURRENT PROCESS's kernel-stack
    # top, written by kstack_install). r8 holds the capped cpu (from the entry APIC block; untouched since).
    # mov rsp, [r8*8+disp32] — 4A 8B 24 C5 <disp32>
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x4A); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x8B); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0x24); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0xC5); eoff = eoff + 4;
    # DISP32-EMIT-WIDTH: store32(+4), NOT store64(+8).
    store32(ep + eoff, kst_base); eoff = eoff + 4;

    # ⭐ push r10 (41 52) — the user RSP saved above, now [ktop-8] of THIS PROCESS's frame (SCF_URSP), which the exit
    # loads RSP from (`mov rsp, [rsp]`) and the arg shuffle loads user_rsp from ([rsp+0x40]). Was
    # `push qword [r8*8 + &pcpu_kernel_rsp_save]` (8 B).
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x52); eoff = eoff + 2;   # push r10

    # 1.42.x (RBP smash, CR2=0x37fed8): preserve the user's RBP across the
    # kstack switch. SYSCALL doesn't save RBP, and the C-ABI syscall_handler
    # (and its ksyscall callees) use RBP as a frame pointer
    # (push rbp; mov rbp,rsp; … ; leave) — so once we `call syscall_handler`
    # RBP holds a KERNEL-stack frame value (~0x37ff00, just under the kstack
    # top 0x3F0000). In ~1/52 boots that leaked-kernel RBP survived back to
    # ring 3 and agnsh's mmap-wrapper epilogue (`mov -0x28(%rbp),%r15`)
    # #PF'd on the smashed RBP while RSP was healthy. We are now on the
    # kernel stack (RSP=kstack), so push the user RBP here and pop it back
    # below right before the user-RSP restore + SYSRET (popped while RSP is
    # still the kstack). Mirrors the existing rcx/r11 save-for-SYSRET block;
    # the push is balanced by the matching pop in the exit path, keeping the
    # SYSRET RSP intact. push rbp — 0x55
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x55); eoff = eoff + 1;

    # S9: Set IBRS (Spectre v2 mitigation) if supported
    # Now on kernel stack so we can use push/pop to preserve regs.
    # push rax; push rcx; push rdx; mov ecx,0x48; xor edx,edx; mov eax,1; wrmsr; pop rdx; pop rcx; pop rax
    if (ibrs_supported == 1) {
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x50); eoff = eoff + 1;  # push rax
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x51); eoff = eoff + 1;  # push rcx
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x52); eoff = eoff + 1;  # push rdx
        # mov ecx, 0x48
        store8(ep + eoff, 0xB9); store32(ep + eoff + 1, 0x48); eoff = eoff + 5;
        # xor edx, edx
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x31); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0xD2); eoff = eoff + 2;
        # mov eax, 1
        store8(ep + eoff, 0xB8); store32(ep + eoff + 1, 1); eoff = eoff + 5;
        # wrmsr
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x0F); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x30); eoff = eoff + 2;
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x5A); eoff = eoff + 1;  # pop rdx
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x59); eoff = eoff + 1;  # pop rcx
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x58); eoff = eoff + 1;  # pop rax
    }

    # Push preserved values for SYSRET
    # push rcx (user RIP)  — 51
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x51); eoff = eoff + 1;
    # push r11 (user RFLAGS) — 41 53
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x53); eoff = eoff + 2;
    # ⭐ 1.57.6 (Path 2, S3.3): push rbx, r12, r13, r14, r15 — the caller's callee-saved set, [ktop-40..-72]
    # (SCF_RBX..SCF_R15). syscall_handler (cyrius #regalloc) preserves them, so the exit's pops are
    # value-identical on a normal return; what the pushes buy is a frame that is AUTHORITATIVE for this
    # process — a syscall switched out mid-flight (a CPL0 preemption window, S3.4's blocking waits) and
    # fork read the caller's resume registers from HERE, not from a per-CPU capture the next syscall on
    # that CPU overwrites. RSP at `call syscall_handler` is now ktop-72 ≡ 8 (mod 16): the same parity the
    # three pushes (ktop-24) gave. 53 / 41 54 / 41 55 / 41 56 / 41 57.
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x53); eoff = eoff + 1;                                         # push rbx
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x54); eoff = eoff + 2;           # push r12
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x55); eoff = eoff + 2;           # push r13
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x56); eoff = eoff + 2;           # push r14
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x57); eoff = eoff + 2;           # push r15

    # Set up args for syscall_handler(user_rsp, sc_num, sc_arg1, sc_arg2, sc_arg3)
    # Current regs: rax=syscall#, rdi=arg1, rsi=arg2, rdx=arg3
    # Need: rdi=user_rsp, rsi=sc_num, rdx=sc_arg1, rcx=sc_arg2, r8=sc_arg3
    # Shuffle carefully:
    #   r8 = rdx (arg3)  — but rdx is arg2 in user convention...
    # Wait — let me use Linux convention: rax=num, rdi=arg1, rsi=arg2, rdx=arg3
    # syscall_handler(user_rsp, num, arg1, arg2, arg3)
    # ABI: rdi=user_rsp, rsi=num, rdx=arg1, rcx=arg2, r8=arg3

    # Save rdx (arg3) to r8 first (before we clobber it)
    # Actually rdx=arg3 in our convention. Let me be precise:
    # User passes: rax=num, rdi=arg1, rsi=arg2, rdx=arg3
    # We need: rdi=user_rsp, rsi=num, rdx=arg1, rcx=arg2, r8=arg3

    # mov r8, rdx    — 49 89 D0  (save arg3)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x49); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x89); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xD0); eoff = eoff + 3;
    # mov rcx, rsi   — 48 89 F1  (arg2 → rcx)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x48); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x89); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xF1); eoff = eoff + 3;
    # mov rdx, rdi   — 48 89 FA  (arg1 → rdx)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x48); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x89); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xFA); eoff = eoff + 3;
    # mov rsi, rax   — 48 89 C6  (num → rsi)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x48); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x89); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xC6); eoff = eoff + 3;
    # ⭐ 1.57.7 (Path 2, S3.4, stub bite C) — user_rsp = [ktop-8] of THIS process's frame, read straight off the stack:
    # `mov rdi, [rsp+0x40]` (48 8B 7C 24 40). RSP here = ktop-72 after the 9 pushes (r10 [ktop-8], rbp, rcx, r11, rbx,
    # r12-r15 [ktop-72]; the IBRS-set block between push rbp and push rcx is push/pop balanced), so +0x40 = ktop-8.
    # This was the "off-100 consumer" (37 B): re-read the APIC into rax, cap it, `mov rdi, [rax*8 + &pcpu_kernel_rsp_save]`
    # — one of the three LAPIC reads per syscall; two remain (entry and exit).
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x48); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x8B); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0x7C); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0x24); store8(ep + eoff + 4, 0x40); eoff = eoff + 5;   # mov rdi, [rsp+0x40]

    # 1.40.3: STAC before the handler — SMAP (CR4.SMAP, set on Zen/-cpu max)
    # blocks supervisor access to user (U/S=1) pages, so write()/read() copying
    # the user buffer faulted (e=0001 on the user msg VA). STAC sets EFLAGS.AC,
    # permitting the handler's user-memory access; CLAC clears it after. (Linux's
    # user-copy model.) STAC/CLAC = 0F 01 CB / 0F 01 CA.
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x0F); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x01); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xCB); eoff = eoff + 3;  # stac
    # call syscall_handler  — mov rax, imm64; call rax
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x48); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0xB8); eoff = eoff + 2;
    store64(ep + eoff, handler); eoff = eoff + 8;
    store8(ep + eoff, 0xFF); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0xD0); eoff = eoff + 2;
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x0F); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x01); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xCA); eoff = eoff + 3;  # clac

    # rax now has return value
    # ⭐ 1.57.6 (Path 2, S3.3): pop r15, r14, r13, r12, rbx — the frame's callee-saved set, reverse order.
    # 41 5F / 41 5E / 41 5D / 41 5C / 5B. Nothing here writes rax (the return value).
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x5F); eoff = eoff + 2;           # pop r15
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x5E); eoff = eoff + 2;           # pop r14
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x5D); eoff = eoff + 2;           # pop r13
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x5C); eoff = eoff + 2;           # pop r12
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x5B); eoff = eoff + 1;                                         # pop rbx

    # Restore r11 (saved RFLAGS) and rcx (return RIP)
    # pop r11 — 41 5B
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x5B); eoff = eoff + 2;
    # pop rcx — 59
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x59); eoff = eoff + 1;

    # 1.42.x (RBP smash): restore the user's RBP, saved on entry just after
    # the kstack switch. We are still on the kernel stack (RSP=kstack) here —
    # the entry order was push rbp; (IBRS bal.); push rcx; push r11, and the
    # exit unwinds pop r11; pop rcx; pop rbp — so this pop sees the user RBP
    # at the bottom of the saved block, with the stack now balanced back to
    # syscall_kstack_top before we reload the user RSP below. pop rbp — 0x5D
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x5D); eoff = eoff + 1;

    # S9: Clear IBRS before returning to user mode (avoid user-mode perf hit).
    #
    # ⛔⛔ 1.56.51 — THIS BLOCK MOVED. It used to be emitted at the very END of the exit sequence,
    # AFTER `mov rsp,[r8*8+krs_base]` had already loaded the caller's RING-3 RSP and after
    # `mov cr3,r10` had installed the user CR3. Its three `push`es are therefore SUPERVISOR (CPL0)
    # data writes through a fully user-controlled RSP — and they happen after the `clac` above has
    # re-armed SMAP. On any CPU with CR4.SMAP set (the boot shim sets it) that is a #PF at CPL0 on
    # the FIRST syscall of the boot: the user stack page is mapped US=1 by proc_map_page, EFLAGS.AC
    # is clear, so idt.cyr's vector-14 stub takes the CPL0 branch and the box wedges with no banner
    # past that point. On a CPU with IBRS but no SMAP it instead silently clobbers 24 bytes of the
    # caller's SysV red zone on EVERY syscall return.
    # ⚠ IT IS LATENT TODAY ONLY BY ACCIDENT, on both substrates agnos runs on: QEMU TCG `-cpu max`
    # reports spec-ctrl=false, and AMD Cezanne advertises IBRS in CPUID Fn8000_0008_EBX[14] rather
    # than leaf-7 EDX[26], which is the only bit syscall_init tests — so ibrs_supported is 0 on
    # archaemenid and this block is not emitted at all. It WOULD be emitted on any Intel part with
    # the Spectre-v2 microcode, or under KVM on Intel.
    # ⭐ Emitted here instead: still on the KERNEL stack (the pops above balanced it back to
    # syscall_kstack_top), so the pushes land in kernel memory. rax (return value) and rcx (user
    # RIP) are live and are saved/restored by the block itself; r11 (user RFLAGS) is untouched.
    # ⚠ REGISTER CONTRACT: clobbers-and-restores rax/rcx/rdx; writes MSR 0x48 (IA32_SPEC_CTRL) = 0;
    # does NOT touch r8, which is what keeps the exit APIC block below the sole writer of r8.
    #
    # ⛔ HONEST LIMIT — VERIFIED BY EMIT ORDER, NOT BY A RUNNING KERNEL, AND THE A/B THAT WAS RUN
    # RETURNED A NULL. What IS established: the pushes are emitted between the `pop rbp` above and
    # the exit reload below, so they land on the kernel stack rather than the caller's ring-3 stack.
    # What is NOT established: that the old position actually faults. Measured at 1.56.51 by
    # forcing `ibrs_supported = 1` (QEMU TCG refuses `-cpu max,+spec-ctrl` — "TCG doesn't support
    # requested feature ... spec-ctrl" — so the flag has to be forced) and running agnsh-smoke
    # THREE times per arm: old position 3/3 booted to the agnsh banner, new position 3/3 booted.
    # The experiment discriminates NOTHING and must not be written up as a confirmation.
    # ⚠ Two single-run observations during that session DID show a missing agnsh banner and were
    # briefly read as the predicted wedge. They were the OVMF hand-off flake; the 3x3 above is what
    # settles it. This is exactly why state.md says to check the kernel banner before believing any
    # verdict — and why one run is never a result here.
    # ⚠ OPEN: why the old position does not fault under TCG is unexplained. The mechanism requires
    # CR4.SMAP set (the boot shim ORs it), a US=1 user stack page (proc_map_page writes 0x87) and
    # EFLAGS.AC clear (the `clac` above) — all three appear to hold, so either TCG does not enforce
    # SMAP on this path or one of the three premises is false. Settling it needs a host whose
    # CPUID leaf-7 EDX[26] is genuinely set: an Intel part with the Spectre-v2 microcode, or KVM on
    # Intel. AMD Cezanne reports IBRS in Fn8000_0008_EBX[14], which syscall_init does not test, so
    # archaemenid cannot serve as that host either.
    if (ibrs_supported == 1) {
        # Save rax (syscall return value), then wrmsr, then restore
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x50); eoff = eoff + 1;  # push rax
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x51); eoff = eoff + 1;  # push rcx (user RIP — save again)
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x52); eoff = eoff + 1;  # push rdx
        # mov ecx, 0x48
        store8(ep + eoff, 0xB9); store32(ep + eoff + 1, 0x48); eoff = eoff + 5;
        # xor edx, edx
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x31); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0xD2); eoff = eoff + 2;
        # xor eax, eax (IBRS=0 to clear)
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x31); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0xC0); eoff = eoff + 2;
        # wrmsr
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x0F); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x30); eoff = eoff + 2;
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x5A); eoff = eoff + 1;  # pop rdx
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x59); eoff = eoff + 1;  # pop rcx
        store8(ep + eoff, 0x58); eoff = eoff + 1;  # pop rax
    }

    # 1.46.x 4b exit APIC block (1.57.6: it no longer feeds the RSP reload — see the ⭐ below — only the
    # user-CR3 load after it). rax holds the syscall RETURN VALUE here, so we re-read the APIC into r8 (EXIT-R8-FREE: r8 is
    # dead post-handler — the arg shuffle's `mov r8,rdx` value died inside the call) — NOT rax.
    # APIC-UNDER-KERNEL-CR3: this runs BEFORE the kernel→user CR3 switch below, so the 0xFEE00020
    # MMIO read resolves under the kernel CR3. Same 7-instruction entry-form block, then a based load.
    # Was `mov r10,imm64(&kernel_rsp_save); mov rsp,[r10]`.
    # mov r8, 0xFEE00020 — 49 B8 imm64
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x49); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0xB8); eoff = eoff + 2;
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x20); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xE0); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0xFE);
    store8(ep + eoff + 4, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 5, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 6, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 7, 0x00); eoff = eoff + 8;
    # mov r8d, [r8] — 45 8B 00
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x45); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x8B); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0x00); eoff = eoff + 3;
    # shr r8, 24 — 49 C1 E8 18
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x49); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0xC1); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xE8); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0x18); eoff = eoff + 4;
    # and r8, 0xFF — 41 81 E0 FF 00 00 00
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x81); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xE0);
    store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0xFF); store8(ep + eoff + 4, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 5, 0x00); store8(ep + eoff + 6, 0x00); eoff = eoff + 7;
    # cmp r8, 4 — 49 83 F8 04
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x49); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x83); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xF8); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0x04); eoff = eoff + 4;
    # jb +3 — 72 03   (JB-DISPLACEMENT: lands exactly past 45 31 C0)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x72); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x03); eoff = eoff + 2;
    # xor r8d, r8d — 45 31 C0
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x45); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x31); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0xC0); eoff = eoff + 3;
    # ⭐⭐ 1.57.6 (Path 2, S3.3): mov rsp, [rsp] — 48 8B 24 24. The pops above (r15..rbx, r11, rcx, rbp) and the
    # balanced IBRS-clear block leave RSP at [ktop-8], this process's saved user RSP (SCF_URSP). This was
    # `mov rsp, [r8*8 + &pcpu_kernel_rsp_save]` — a PER-CPU cell the next syscall on this CPU overwrites, so a
    # syscall switched out mid-flight, or resumed on another CPU, would have SYSRET'd on another process's
    # stack (M-B1 puts it back and KSTACK_SELFTEST's distinct-RSP probes go red). r8 is not written between
    # the APIC block above and the user-CR3 load below.
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x48); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x8B); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0x24); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0x24); eoff = eoff + 4;

    # S8 (KPTI): Switch from kernel CR3 back to user CR3 before SYSRET.
    # 1.46.x 4c POINT 6: load THIS CPU's user CR3 from pcpu_kpti_user_cr3, install it. r8 was RE-READ by
    # the 4b exit APIC block just above (the exit reload) and nothing between that block and here writes
    # r8 (objdump-confirmed: the exit reload's `mov rsp,[r8*8+disp32]` is the last r8 use). 1.56.51: the
    # IBRS-clear block used to run AFTER this and now runs BEFORE the APIC block — it touches only
    # rax/rcx/rdx, so it is not an r8 writer either way and this invariant is strengthened, not broken.
    # The SIB load folds the old indirection. Was 3 instr; now: mov r10,[r8*8 +
    # &pcpu_kpti_user_cr3]; mov cr3,r10.
    # mov r10, [r8*8+disp32] — 4E 8B 14 C5 <disp32>
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x4E); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x8B); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0x14); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0xC5); eoff = eoff + 4;
    # DISP32-EMIT-WIDTH: store32(+4), NOT store64(+8). disp = the single u64 base &pcpu_kpti_user_cr3.
    store32(ep + eoff, ucr3_addr); eoff = eoff + 4;
    # mov cr3, r10 — 41 0F 22 DA (REX.B for r10; NOT 0x44 = REX.R → cr11 → #UD)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x41); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x0F); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0x22); store8(ep + eoff + 3, 0xDA); eoff = eoff + 4;

    # (S9's IBRS-clear block used to sit HERE and was moved up, above the RSP/CR3 restore —
    # see the ⛔⛔ note at its new site for why this position was a CPL0 write to the user stack.)

    # sysretq: atomically restores RIP from RCX, RFLAGS from R11,
    # CS = STAR[63:48]+16 = 0x23 (user CS; CPL/CS.RPL forced to 3), SS = STAR[63:48]+8 = 0x1B (user SS).
    # SS.RPL=3 comes from STAR[63:48]=0x13 being RPL-baked, NOT from a SYSRET force: AMD does NOT force
    # SS.RPL (APM Vol 3 §SYSRET), so a base lacking RPL bits (0x10) yields SS=0x18/RPL0 → the next ring-3
    # iretq #GP(0x18)'s on real Zen. See syscall_msr_init's STAR write for the full root-cause note.
    # sysretq (48 0F 07)
    store8(ep + eoff, 0x48); store8(ep + eoff + 1, 0x0F); store8(ep + eoff + 2, 0x07); eoff = eoff + 3;

    # ⛔⛔ 1.56.54 — THIS EMITTER HAD NO BOUND CHECK AT ALL, into a FIXED buffer. `syscall_entry_buf` is
    # `var …[256]` at MODULE scope = 256 u64 = **2048 bytes**, and `eoff` was never compared against it
    # anywhere in this function. Every byte past the end would land in whatever .bss follows — silently,
    # and on the one code path every syscall in the system runs through, so the corruption would present
    # as an unrelated global going wrong long after boot.
    # ⚠ It has never overflowed; the point is that nothing said so. This cut ADDS 16 bytes to the stub
    # (the user RIP/RFLAGS saves for `fork`#96), which is exactly the kind of growth that eventually
    # finds a ceiling nobody is watching — the IBRS block already forced one enlargement, per the
    # buffer`s own comment.
    # ⭐ Print the size unconditionally. A number in the boot log is what makes the next growth`s headroom
    # a fact rather than an assumption, and it costs one line on a path that runs once.
    kprint("syscall: stub ", 14); kprint_num(eoff); kprintln(" bytes of 2048", 14);
    # 1.57.6 (S3.3): the size ORACLE is keyed on this — 280 bytes with ibrs=0 (258 + 8 + 9 + 9 - 4); 1.57.7 (S3.4 stub
    # bite C): 174 (280 - 63 capture - 5 save - 6 push - 32 off-100). The IBRS
    # blocks add bytes on a host whose CPUID leaf-7 EDX[26] is set, so a size check must read this line too.
    kprint("syscall: ibrs=", 14); kprint_num(ibrs_supported); kprintln("", 0);
    if (eoff > 2048) {
        kprintln("syscall: STUB OVERFLOWED ITS BUFFER - .bss past syscall_entry_buf is CORRUPT", 76);
        arch_halt();
    }
    return 0;
}
